WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.903 --> 00:00:07.692
A worker never writes to your checkout.

2
00:00:07.692 --> 00:00:13.126
It works in a git worktree of its own, and when it sends its done, what that

3
00:00:13.126 --> 00:00:16.916
worktree holds shows up as a change, waiting for you.

4
00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:22.779
Let us speed the simulation up until a few workers are done, then pause it, so the

5
00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:23.994
list holds still.

6
00:00:29.146 --> 00:00:36.616
g c goes to the first change waiting, under the commands and questions; enter opens

7
00:00:36.616 --> 00:00:36.886
it.

8
00:00:36.886 --> 00:00:44.087
Its first line is the verdict: what ran on its tree, whether it stays inside its

9
00:00:44.087 --> 00:00:48.947
card's paths, whether it can be approved as it stands.

10
00:00:48.947 --> 00:00:56.327
A change of several files lists them above its patch, with the lines each adds and

11
00:00:56.327 --> 00:01:03.707
removes, and when they are of more than one kind, the lines by kind first: source,

12
00:01:03.707 --> 00:01:05.237
tests, generated.

13
00:01:05.237 --> 00:01:12.527
j and k scroll; the brackets jump from file to file; w wraps the long lines under

14
00:01:12.527 --> 00:01:13.517
themselves.

15
00:01:14.719 --> 00:01:19.055
c puts a note on the line at the top of the view.

16
00:01:19.055 --> 00:01:26.400
Scroll a line of a hunk to the top, press c, write, and enter keeps it; the line is

17
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.019
marked.

18
00:01:27.019 --> 00:01:29.497
A second note, further down.

19
00:01:29.497 --> 00:01:35.691
The notes belong to this patch: they stay with it until you send them.

20
00:01:43.594 --> 00:01:49.725
n sends the notes to the worker in one message, each under its file and line with

21
00:01:49.725 --> 00:01:55.781
the line itself quoted, and the change goes back like a reject: not applied, the

22
00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:58.127
worker goes on with your notes.

23
00:01:58.127 --> 00:02:01.685
It is the third answer beside apply and reject.

24
00:02:02.886 --> 00:02:07.026
Back on Home, g c and enter open the next change.

25
00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:12.349
x chooses the hunk at the top; a check mark goes on its header.

26
00:02:12.349 --> 00:02:19.361
a then applies only the chosen hunks, staged in your checkout, and the rest is read

27
00:02:19.361 --> 00:02:24.177
again from the worktree and waits as a change of its own.

28
00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:35.942
Another change.

29
00:02:35.942 --> 00:02:42.004
The greater-than key opens a message to its worker with the hunk at the top quoted

30
00:02:42.004 --> 00:02:46.070
under your line, in a diff fence with its file's names.

31
00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.805
Write the question above it and send.

32
00:03:07.443 --> 00:03:14.554
And a change you do not want: r rejects it and opens the composer addressed to its

33
00:03:14.554 --> 00:03:16.549
worker, so you say why.

34
00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:23.313
The worker reads your reason and tries another way; nothing of it touched your

35
00:03:23.313 --> 00:03:24.094
checkout.

36
00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:40.347
Let the simulation run again.

37
00:03:40.347 --> 00:03:44.021
Changes are not the only thing to decide.

38
00:03:44.021 --> 00:03:51.278
A flag is a command Claude Code would have asked you about: routine commands, the

39
00:03:51.278 --> 00:03:58.267
build and the tests, run on their own; the rest wait at the top of Home, shown

40
00:03:58.267 --> 00:04:01.582
whole, for a to allow or r to refuse.

41
00:04:01.582 --> 00:04:08.032
A question mark is a question an agent asked you, its answers as digits.

42
00:04:08.032 --> 00:04:12.960
Capital S puts the item at hand off and opens the next.

43
00:04:14.161 --> 00:04:19.052
That is the gate: nothing reaches your checkout without your yes.

